WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.197
This walkthrough follows the live Fire Toolshed
apps from hydrant testing

2
00:00:03.197 --> 00:00:06.862
through fire flow, sprinkler demand, pump
selection, and tank sizing.

3
00:00:07.544 --> 00:00:10.136
We will use a warehouse example and explain the
project options.

4
00:00:10.955 --> 00:00:12.943
Each app's Help or Guide provides a field
reference.

5
00:00:14.281 --> 00:00:15.217
Start at the hub.

6
00:00:15.977 --> 00:00:18.160
Combined means the pump serves hydrants and
sprinklers.

7
00:00:18.647 --> 00:00:22.137
Separate keeps hydrants on city supply and sizes
sprinklers independently.

8
00:00:22.975 --> 00:00:25.996
Adequate supply skips the pump and tank when
verified supply meets

9
00:00:25.996 --> 00:00:29.447
the actual demands. Completing these apps does
not establish a tank

10
00:00:29.447 --> 00:00:29.876
requirement.

11
00:00:31.065 --> 00:00:32.449
Enter the shared project name.

12
00:00:33.092 --> 00:00:35.431
The top strip shows captured values and
timestamps.

13
00:00:36.016 --> 00:00:37.849
Captures stay in this browser and device.

14
00:00:38.823 --> 00:00:41.806
Navigation alone does not replace another app's
inputs: use its

15
00:00:41.806 --> 00:00:46.153
import action. Also check the project
identification fields inside each report.

16
00:00:47.315 --> 00:00:51.155
In Flow Test Report, enter project, address,
test date and time,

17
00:00:51.526 --> 00:00:55.444
preparer, and witness. Choose suppression supply
for a design supply curve,

18
00:00:55.444 --> 00:00:57.549
or hydrant rating for available capacity.

19
00:00:58.368 --> 00:01:02.110
Choose Benham, MeadHunt, AI and P, Haskell by
state, or an

20
00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:03.046
uploaded report logo.

21
00:01:04.148 --> 00:01:07.852
Identify the residual hydrant, select the number
of separate flow hydrants,

22
00:01:08.105 --> 00:01:09.041
and enter their identifiers.

23
00:01:09.821 --> 00:01:12.257
Layout notes describe positions and flow
direction.

24
00:01:13.057 --> 00:01:15.630
Zero represents the less preferred single-
hydrant test.

25
00:01:16.429 --> 00:01:20.328
Enter both gauge calibration dates and optional
equipment identifiers for traceability.

26
00:01:21.423 --> 00:01:22.924
Static is no-flow pressure.

27
00:01:23.568 --> 00:01:26.414
Residual is pressure while the recorded total
discharge is flowing.

28
00:01:27.115 --> 00:01:31.036
We use seventy-two psi static and forty-eight
psi residual

29
00:01:31.036 --> 00:01:33.102
at twelve hundred fifty-six gallons per minute.

30
00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:37.703
Twenty psi is the capacity reference, not the
required sprinkler pressure.

31
00:01:38.783 --> 00:01:42.286
For pitot-derived flow, enter pressure and
outlet diameter.

32
00:01:43.124 --> 00:01:46.282
Select actual outlet geometry or a documented
custom coefficient.

33
00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:49.148
Multi-outlet mode measures each open outlet.

34
00:01:50.025 --> 00:01:51.818
Additional open butts is a rough multiplier.

35
00:01:52.835 --> 00:01:55.984
Monster Pitot fixes the coefficient at point
nine and rounds the

36
00:01:55.984 --> 00:01:56.218
result.

37
00:01:57.367 --> 00:02:00.723
Calculate GPM from pitot writes flow into the
test.

38
00:02:01.308 --> 00:02:03.374
Check only procedure steps actually performed.

39
00:02:04.057 --> 00:02:08.443
Record main size, weather, operators, previous
and next test dates, ordinary-

40
00:02:08.443 --> 00:02:10.587
demand confirmation, and unusual conditions.

41
00:02:11.639 --> 00:02:13.959
Optional maps and captioned photos become report
attachments.

42
00:02:15.289 --> 00:02:17.979
Review pressure drop, missing records, and the
curve.

43
00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:22.004
This example estimates nineteen hundred seven
gallons per minute at twenty

44
00:02:22.004 --> 00:02:26.498
psi. A favorable numeric result does not clear
documentation warnings.

45
00:02:27.161 --> 00:02:30.436
Capture saves the supply; Capture and Open also
navigates.

46
00:02:31.157 --> 00:02:32.853
Continue through Fire Flow for the full path.

47
00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:37.321
In Fire Flow, select NFPA One or Florida, I F C

48
00:02:37.321 --> 00:02:38.763
Appendix B, or U F C.

49
00:02:38.763 --> 00:02:42.506
Select edition label, construction group, and
fire-flow calculation area.

50
00:02:43.286 --> 00:02:46.112
Verify that area and the Toolshed lookup against
the project's

51
00:02:46.112 --> 00:02:47.224
adopted table and amendments.

52
00:02:48.576 --> 00:02:50.467
Select the actual sprinkler system.

53
00:02:51.091 --> 00:02:53.664
Quick-response throughout affects the NFPA One
minimum.

54
00:02:54.268 --> 00:02:57.797
Paired table-flow and duration overrides require
adopted-table values.

55
00:02:58.596 --> 00:03:02.319
UFC special-facility choices cover housing,
vehicle parking, and aircraft.

56
00:03:03.235 --> 00:03:06.705
These criteria paths apply different reductions
and limits; do not interchange

57
00:03:06.705 --> 00:03:07.134
them.

58
00:03:08.097 --> 00:03:11.450
Our twenty-five-thousand-square-foot, Type Two
B, sprinklered example

59
00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:14.861
returns one thousand gallons per minute for two
hours: one hundred

60
00:03:14.861 --> 00:03:18.682
twenty thousand gallons. Optional sprinkler flow
and duration support comparison.

61
00:03:19.657 --> 00:03:21.937
Click Capture for Pump and Tank and acknowledge
the confirmation.

62
00:03:23.347 --> 00:03:27.636
Complete building, preparer, date, and
assumptions in Sprinkler Estimator.

63
00:03:28.104 --> 00:03:31.768
Simple mode accepts a known total flow; document
included hose demand.

64
00:03:32.373 --> 00:03:35.004
Criteria-assisted develops flow from hazard
criteria.

65
00:03:35.726 --> 00:03:38.864
Storage and E S F R compares the specific NFPA
and

66
00:03:38.864 --> 00:03:40.384
FM arrangements listed in the app.

67
00:03:41.431 --> 00:03:46.323
In criteria-assisted mode, select NFPA Thirteen,
Florida, UFC, Air Force

68
00:03:46.323 --> 00:03:49.247
Sundown, or FM, then wet or dry and the
governing hazard.

69
00:03:50.203 --> 00:03:51.606
Available presets change with framework.

70
00:03:52.347 --> 00:03:56.362
Check seeded values, dry-area adjustments, wet-
only cases, and UFC

71
00:03:56.362 --> 00:03:58.448
height categories against project criteria.

72
00:03:59.455 --> 00:04:02.593
Point-two density times fifteen hundred square
feet gives three hundred

73
00:04:02.593 --> 00:04:05.985
gallons per minute. Add the enabled two-hundred-
fifty-gallon hose

74
00:04:05.985 --> 00:04:07.759
allowance for five hundred fifty total.

75
00:04:08.519 --> 00:04:10.312
Auto duration follows framework presets.

76
00:04:11.112 --> 00:04:14.523
Our manually entered sixty minutes is a training
assumption, recorded in

77
00:04:14.523 --> 00:04:15.264
the duration basis.

78
00:04:16.455 --> 00:04:20.197
Enter elevation from pump discharge to highest
head, K factor, and

79
00:04:20.197 --> 00:04:23.765
coverage. A head-flow override replaces density
times coverage.

80
00:04:24.642 --> 00:04:28.423
Automatic head pressure uses flow divided by K,
squared, subject to

81
00:04:28.423 --> 00:04:31.347
the minimum floor. K eight and twenty-six
gallons per minute

82
00:04:31.347 --> 00:04:33.283
give ten-point-six psi.

83
00:04:34.371 --> 00:04:38.173
Select backflow type or custom pressure loss,
planning margin, and other

84
00:04:38.173 --> 00:04:42.656
losses. Hazen-Williams inputs are diameter,
length, C factor, and fitting

85
00:04:42.656 --> 00:04:45.307
equivalent length for lead-in, riser, and roof
main.

86
00:04:45.911 --> 00:04:48.465
Calculated friction replaces matching fixed
allowances.

87
00:04:49.479 --> 00:04:50.551
Avoid counting losses twice.

88
00:04:51.788 --> 00:04:56.486
Enable only applicable equipment: riser
assembly, zone valve, meter, and other

89
00:04:56.486 --> 00:04:58.961
documented losses. Record the loss methodology.

90
00:04:59.917 --> 00:05:03.075
Click Use saved flow test, check the imported
readings, and enable

91
00:05:03.075 --> 00:05:06.369
the overlay. Compare supply and demand at the
same flow and

92
00:05:06.369 --> 00:05:07.051
reference location.

93
00:05:08.205 --> 00:05:11.265
Our completed demand is five hundred fifty
gallons per minute at

94
00:05:11.265 --> 00:05:14.054
sixty-four-point-seven psi for sixty minutes.

95
00:05:14.872 --> 00:05:18.577
Estimated supply is sixty-six-point-eight psi: a
small preliminary

96
00:05:18.577 --> 00:05:22.573
margin. Review the stack and actual conditions
before deciding a pump

97
00:05:22.573 --> 00:05:25.536
is needed. Capture for Pump Sizer saves this
demand.

98
00:05:26.705 --> 00:05:31.851
Storage mode needs commodity, storage and
ceiling heights, K, orientation, response,

99
00:05:31.851 --> 00:05:36.237
and fire arrangement. Check each method's heads,
pressure, hose, and

100
00:05:36.237 --> 00:05:40.993
duration. Record edition, FM revision, and
references; choose the governing result.

101
00:05:41.636 --> 00:05:42.787
Built-in cases are limited.

102
00:05:43.566 --> 00:05:45.866
Recheck retained pressure inputs when changing
modes.

103
00:05:46.974 --> 00:05:49.781
In Pump Sizer, click Import Sprinkler Demand
first.

104
00:05:50.424 --> 00:05:55.005
Verify imported test readings, flow, pressure,
and duration; capture pills alone

105
00:05:55.005 --> 00:05:56.233
do not prove the form is current.

106
00:05:57.071 --> 00:05:58.962
Import Fire Flow supports that demand alone.

107
00:05:59.683 --> 00:06:02.627
Pump Serves Both combines the saved demands into
a planning envelope.

108
00:06:03.807 --> 00:06:06.740
Our actual duties are one thousand gallons per
minute at twenty

109
00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:11.127
psi, and five hundred fifty at sixty-four-point-
seven psi.

110
00:06:11.645 --> 00:06:13.848
Pump Serves Both takes the larger flow and
pressure.

111
00:06:14.433 --> 00:06:18.273
That envelope does not establish simultaneous
flow; retain actual duty points

112
00:06:18.273 --> 00:06:19.910
and verify project demand rules.

113
00:06:21.057 --> 00:06:23.084
Choose hydrant curve or direct suction.

114
00:06:23.767 --> 00:06:26.944
Curve mode subtracts entered backflow, pipe, and
elevation losses.

115
00:06:27.626 --> 00:06:29.926
Direct mode takes minimum and maximum suction.

116
00:06:30.511 --> 00:06:32.188
Maximum static checks churn discharge.

117
00:06:32.987 --> 00:06:35.599
A tank-fed pump needs tank-level and suction-
loss conditions;

118
00:06:35.989 --> 00:06:37.353
remove municipal pressure credit.

119
00:06:38.474 --> 00:06:41.763
At the combined envelope, municipal supply is
about fifty-six-point-

120
00:06:41.763 --> 00:06:45.474
three psi, leaving eight-point-four psi boost.

121
00:06:46.156 --> 00:06:49.177
This is conservative compared with checking the
actual duties separately.

122
00:06:50.133 --> 00:06:51.926
Required discharge and net pump pressure differ.

123
00:06:52.764 --> 00:06:56.254
Additional demand rows take names, flows, and
discharge pressures for other

124
00:06:56.254 --> 00:06:56.507
cases.

125
00:06:57.724 --> 00:07:01.330
Enter proposed rated flow, net pressure, and
certified churn pressure.

126
00:07:01.934 --> 00:07:06.439
Our one-thousand-gallon, seventy-psi rating and
ninety-psi churn

127
00:07:06.439 --> 00:07:10.123
are illustrative. Choose a certified point or at
least three ascending-

128
00:07:10.123 --> 00:07:14.334
flow curve points. Identify net versus discharge
pressure so suction is

129
00:07:14.334 --> 00:07:14.860
treated correctly.

130
00:07:16.065 --> 00:07:19.807
Electric mode uses voltage, locked-rotor
current, and other motor or

131
00:07:19.807 --> 00:07:20.977
accessory full-load current.

132
00:07:21.679 --> 00:07:25.246
Diesel adds engine, runtime, consumption, and
fuel-storage planning.

133
00:07:25.870 --> 00:07:27.351
Apply UFC mode when appropriate.

134
00:07:28.189 --> 00:07:31.737
Coordinate these estimates with electrical and
mechanical engineers and the selected

135
00:07:31.737 --> 00:07:32.458
equipment data.

136
00:07:33.574 --> 00:07:36.108
On Verify and Curve, check rated and one-
hundred-fifty-percent

137
00:07:36.673 --> 00:07:39.987
flow, churn, maximum discharge, and every demand
point.

138
00:07:40.572 --> 00:07:43.730
A pass depends on entered or assumed data;
confirm the manufacturer

139
00:07:43.730 --> 00:07:48.817
curve. Pump Room Size provides a preliminary
layout; coordinate equipment, access,

140
00:07:49.227 --> 00:07:50.884
electrical clearance, and diesel provisions.

141
00:07:52.159 --> 00:07:55.648
Return to System Calculation and Supply, then
Capture for Tank or

142
00:07:55.648 --> 00:07:58.435
Capture and Open. Preserve both upstream
demands.

143
00:07:59.215 --> 00:08:02.237
Pump flow and tank volume can have different
governing scenarios: a

144
00:08:02.237 --> 00:08:05.239
smaller flow over a longer duration may require
more gallons than

145
00:08:05.239 --> 00:08:05.843
the largest flow.

146
00:08:06.909 --> 00:08:09.950
Use Governing Demand compares fire-flow and
sprinkler captures.

147
00:08:10.612 --> 00:08:14.238
Import Upstream prefers that comparison, with
other captures as fallbacks.

148
00:08:14.862 --> 00:08:18.215
Individual buttons import pump, fire-flow, or
sprinkler demand.

149
00:08:18.955 --> 00:08:21.119
Verify the driver note, flow, and duration.

150
00:08:22.094 --> 00:08:25.427
Here, one hundred twenty thousand gallons
exceeds thirty-three thousand.

151
00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:29.802
The tank's NFPA One panel offers another fire-
flow calculation.

152
00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:35.533
Edition, construction, area, sprinklers, quick-
response, and paired overrides mirror the

153
00:08:35.533 --> 00:08:38.613
earlier app. Apply to tank demand replaces
demand fields.

154
00:08:39.529 --> 00:08:43.330
Avoid overwriting a saved calculation with
unrelated entries; recheck the driver

155
00:08:43.330 --> 00:08:43.623
note.

156
00:08:44.711 --> 00:08:46.407
Enter the number of identical tanks.

157
00:08:47.225 --> 00:08:51.358
NFPA, UFC, and FM planning frameworks default to
five, ten, and

158
00:08:51.358 --> 00:08:55.217
fifteen percent contingency; these are app
defaults, not universal code additions.

159
00:08:56.075 --> 00:08:57.518
Blank override retains the default.

160
00:08:58.258 --> 00:09:02.001
Unusable allowance separately accounts for water
unavailable as effective storage.

161
00:09:03.211 --> 00:09:06.466
One hundred twenty thousand gallons plus five
percent gives a one-

162
00:09:06.466 --> 00:09:08.532
hundred-twenty-six-thousand-gallon usable
target.

163
00:09:08.981 --> 00:09:12.723
Divide by point nine five for five percent
unusable volume: approximately

164
00:09:12.723 --> 00:09:15.901
one hundred thirty-two thousand six hundred
thirty-two shell gallons.

165
00:09:16.622 --> 00:09:18.825
Confirm effective capacity with the final
geometry.

166
00:09:19.971 --> 00:09:23.148
Enter actual height and diameter limits; zero
means unlimited.

167
00:09:23.986 --> 00:09:26.832
Pad extension is each side: two feet adds four
feet to

168
00:09:26.832 --> 00:09:29.834
diameter. Thickness estimates concrete quantity
only.

169
00:09:30.302 --> 00:09:36.014
Coordinate setbacks, access, overhead limits,
geotechnical conditions, and tank manufacturer
requirements.

170
00:09:37.304 --> 00:09:40.325
The selected example is thirty-six feet across
and eighteen feet

171
00:09:40.325 --> 00:09:43.600
high: about one hundred thirty-seven thousand
fifty-six shell gallons,

172
00:09:43.873 --> 00:09:44.848
with a forty-foot pad.

173
00:09:45.511 --> 00:09:47.168
Check spare volume and constraint status.

174
00:09:48.064 --> 00:09:51.359
Catalog rows are planning sizes; select an
eligible row and confirm

175
00:09:51.359 --> 00:09:52.821
manufacturer usable capacity.

176
00:09:53.971 --> 00:09:57.694
Lower height limits favor wider or multiple
tanks; tight footprints may

177
00:09:57.694 --> 00:10:01.826
require taller tanks. Multiple tanks do not
automatically establish redundancy.

178
00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:05.140
Custom diameter and height calculate cylinder
volume.

179
00:10:05.862 --> 00:10:09.624
When prefab sizes fail, review field-erected
screening options and constraint

180
00:10:09.624 --> 00:10:10.618
warnings with the supplier.

181
00:10:11.804 --> 00:10:15.215
Use Clean Report or Print, then Save Report for
available HTML

182
00:10:15.215 --> 00:10:18.880
and project data. Save tank handoff preserves
the selection.

183
00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:22.545
Keep source tests, criteria, assumptions, and
captures.

184
00:10:23.461 --> 00:10:25.449
Verify project identity and timestamps.

185
00:10:26.346 --> 00:10:31.005
Final hydraulics, certified curves, effective
storage, and approval remain separate
engineering

186
00:10:31.005 --> 00:10:31.648
deliverables.
